From: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
Date: Sun, 9 Nov 2026 16:50:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] phy: airoha: usb: only program the SerDes mux for the shared
 port

SSTR.USB_PCIE_SEL selects the mode of the *shared* SerDes, the one described
as serdes-port = AIROHA_SCU_SERDES_USB2.  airoha_usb_phy_u3_set_mode() wrote
that bit unconditionally, for every instance, so a request coming from the
dedicated USB-only SerDes (AIROHA_SCU_SERDES_USB1) still reprogrammed the
shared one.

That is not a harmless no-op.  On AN7581 pcie2 is wired to the shared SerDes
(an7581.dtsi: pcie2 phys = <&usb1_phy ...>, and usb1_phy's serdes-port is
AIROHA_SCU_SERDES_USB2) and carries the second Wi-Fi chip, i.e. the 6 GHz
radio.  usb_add_hcd() calls usb_phy_roothub_set_mode(PHY_MODE_USB_HOST_SS)
for every PHY listed in the controller's "phys" property, so the moment a USB
controller on the USB1 SerDes is enabled, this write switches the shared
SerDes away from PCIe and takes the pcie2 link down underneath a live driver.

Keep the mux write for the shared port only.  The dedicated port has no mux
to program, so U3 mode selection is simply a no-op there; asking it for PCIe
mode is still rejected as invalid.

This is a latent defect: usb0 is status = "disabled" on this board today, so
nothing reaches the write yet.  It becomes live as soon as usb0 is enabled,
which is exactly the change wanted to give the board a second, independent
radio path.

---
--- a/drivers/phy/airoha/phy-airoha-usb.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/airoha/phy-airoha-usb.c
@@ -418,10 +418,20 @@ static int airoha_usb_phy_u3_set_mode(st
 {
 	u32 sel;
 
-	/* Only USB2 supports PCIe mode */
-	if (mode == PHY_MODE_PCIE &&
-	    priv->serdes_port != AIROHA_SCU_SERDES_USB2)
-		return -EINVAL;
+	/*
+	 * Only the shared SerDes (USB2) is muxed between PCIe and USB.
+	 * USB1 has a dedicated USB-only SerDes, so there is no mux to program
+	 * from that port -- and writing it anyway is not harmless:
+	 * SSTR.USB_PCIE_SEL selects the mode of *USB2*, so a USB1 request would
+	 * steal the shared SerDes from PCIe.  On AN7581 pcie2 rides on that
+	 * SerDes (it carries the second Wi-Fi chip, i.e. the 6 GHz radio), so
+	 * flipping it at runtime takes that link down.
+	 */
+	if (priv->serdes_port != AIROHA_SCU_SERDES_USB2) {
+		if (mode == PHY_MODE_PCIE)
+			return -EINVAL;
+		return 0;
+	}
 
 	if (mode == PHY_MODE_PCIE)
 		sel = AIROHA_SCU_SSTR_USB_PCIE_SEL_PCIE;
